Agreed. A text sweep would only find the obvious spellings and miss the dangerous cases hidden behind aliases, generics, inferred object literals, helper returns, Drizzle row types, and barrel imports.

I’m expanding this into a semantic trust-boundary audit with a closed denominator:

- every branded type definition and parser/mint;
- every `$type<T>` branded database column and every producer that writes it;
- every `castId`, `as`, double cast, generic assertion, suppression, and waiver that can manufacture or erase a brand;
- untrusted ingress from imports, JSON, plugins, provider responses, RPC, environment/config, and database reads;
- downstream places where a branded value selects a registry row, credential, route, tenant-owned entity, or persistence key;
- named negative tests proving bad strings are rejected at the actual producer boundary.

I’ll use ts-morph/TypeScript symbol resolution and ast-grep TS+TSX corroboration, with literal search only as a cross-check. I’ll separate legitimate foreign opaque IDs from values that are being laundered into an Orb brand.

